        <header class="head">
            <h1>Band Biography</h1>
        </header>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/inicio.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo">At the beginning of 1981, influenced by the New Wave Of British Heavy Metal (NWOBHM), Lars placed an ad in The Recycler newspaper trying to find other musicians so he could form a band. James, in response to the ad, meets Lars, and they decide to form a band together.
            <br>Both thought of several names for the band until Lars "stole" the name Metallica from a friend of his, a fanzine editor who was looking for a name for his magazine and suggested the band's current name.<br><br>
            At the end of 1981, Metallica put their first song (Hit the Lights) on the Metal Massacre 1 compilation. At that time, the band's lineup included James Hetfield on rhythm guitar, bass, and vocals, Lars Ulrich on drums, and Lloyd Grant on lead guitar.<br><br>
            In 1982, Ron McGovney, bassist, and Dave Mustaine, lead guitarist, joined the band.<br><br>
            That same year, Metallica recorded their first demo with 4 songs (Hit the Lights, The Mechanix, Jump in the Fire, and Motorbreath), later known as "Power Metal." Shortly after, they recorded another demo (No Life 'Till Leather) with re-recordings of the songs from the first demo plus Metal Militia, Seek and Destroy, and Phantom Lord.<br><br>
            Also in 1982, James and Lars first saw Cliff Burton at a Trauma show. Impressed with the way Cliff played, they decided he should be Metallica's bassist (bassist Ron McGovney had fought with James at the time) and invited him to join the band. At first, he refused the offer but agreed some time later on the condition that Metallica moved from Los Angeles to San Francisco.<br><br>
            In 1983, Metallica finished moving to San Francisco and now with Cliff, recorded two more songs: No Remorse and Whiplash.<br><br>
            While in New York, James and Lars decided to kick Dave Mustaine out of the band, citing excessive drinking. They replaced him with Kirk Hammett, guitarist of Exodus.<br>
        </p>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/inicio2.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo">They then recorded Kill'em All, Metallica's first official album, produced by Paul Curci. The original album title was "Metal Up Your Ass," which was rejected for sounding too "crude." Although the initial album sales were not great, the record played an important role in consolidating the band's loyal fan base in the underground metal scene.<br><br>
            A year later, in 1984, Metallica released their second album, Ride the Lightning, produced by Metallica along with Flemming Rasmussen and recorded in Copenhagen. The album shows a more mature band with more complex compositions. The inclusion of acoustic and slower passages set the band apart from other thrash metal bands of the time.<br><br>
            In 1985, after signing with Elektra Records, Master of Puppets was finished being recorded and mixed in 1986, then released. With the same producer as Ride the Lightning, Metallica confirmed their thrash metal style that they had been playing since Kill'em All. Master of Puppets is considered by many to be the band's best album and one of the best metal albums of all time.<br><br>
            Also in 1986, on September 27, Cliff Burton died in a tour bus accident near Ljungby, Sweden. His tragic death left the band's future uncertain. With the support of Burton's family and knowing Cliff would want the band to continue, the remaining three members of Metallica began auditions to find a new bassist. Jason Newsted joined the band as Burton's replacement, facing numerous comparisons.<br><br>
            In 1987, to announce the new member, the band released the EP Garage Days Re-Revisited with covers of other bands that influenced Metallica.</p>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/inicio1.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo">In 1988, they released their fourth album ...And Justice For All, which featured the band's first music video, One. The album includes some of the band's most structurally complex songs and was nominated for the first time in 1989 for a Grammy (Best Hard Rock/Metal Performance, Vocal, or Instrumental).<br><br>
            In 1989, for the first time, Metallica played in Brazil as part of the Damaged Justice tour.<br><br>
            In 1991, the album Metallica (known as the Black Album) was released, presenting some differences in the band's sound. The Black Album launched Metallica into the mainstream, and various songs from the album were extensively played on the radio worldwide. Much of the album's composition process can be seen in the documentary A Year and a Half in the Life of Metallica.<br><br>
            In 1993, Metallica returned to Brazil.<br><br>
            That same year, the Live Shit: Binge and Purge box set was released, containing a tour diary, 3 CDs, 2 video shows, a stencil, and a Snake Pit Pass.<br><br>
            In 1996, the album Load was released with a significantly different sound from previous albums, and the band members appeared with short hair. Additionally, many fans from Metallica's pre-Black Album era accused the band of "selling out to the media."<br><br>
            Load was originally intended to be a double album, and the unreleased songs resulted in Reload (1997), following the same pattern as the original album.<br><br>
            In 1998, Metallica released Garage Inc., their first official double CD. CD 1 contains 11 unreleased covers, and CD 2 includes covers from the EP Creeping Death/Garage Days 84, Garage Day 87, and B-sides from some singles.<br><br>
            After 6 years, Metallica returned to Brazil for the The Garage Remains The Same tour in 1999.<br><br>
            In April 1999, Metallica performed a concert with Michael Kamen and the San Francisco Symphony Orchestra. This show resulted in a live double CD (called S&M), released in November 1999, containing two unreleased songs: No Leaf Clover and - Human.<br><br>
            In 2000, the song I Disappear was released exclusively for the movie Mission: Impossible 2.<br><br>
            In January 2001, Jason announced his departure from the band, citing personal reasons and physical exhaustion from the years with Metallica. In later interviews, Newsted made it clear that his departure was mainly due to disagreements with Hetfield, who did not allow Jason to dedicate himself to projects outside of Metallica.<br><br>
            Also in 2001, James Hetfield checked into a rehab clinic to address addictions to alcohol and other substances. For about a year, Metallica simply ceased to function. Ulrich and Hammett, for the first time, seriously considered the possibility that Metallica might cease to exist.<br><br>
            A year later, Hetfield left the clinic and returned to the band, and work began to slowly return to normal. Recording for what would become the band's 8th album continued, with the band as a trio. The bass parts were handled by producer Bob Rock.</p><br>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/inicio3.jpg" style="height:410px">
        <p class="paragrafo">After more than 2 years without a bassist, in February 2003, Metallica announced the new band member: Robert Trujillo.</br><br>
            In June 2003, Metallica released their 8th studio album: St. Anger, with long songs, no solos, and poor production. The album reflects the turbulent period the band went through, as documented in the film "Some Kind of Monster".</br><br>
            After nearly a decade and a half of working together, Metallica decided that the production of their ninth studio album would not be handled by Bob Rock, who had been producing the band's records since 1991. The chosen producer was Rick Rubin, who had previously worked with Slayer, Red Hot Chili Peppers, and System of a Down.
            Due to the harsh criticism received by the band after St. Anger and the promise of an old-school sound, there was great anticipation for the release of this new album. As part of a campaign to promote the album, Mission: Metallica, a site showcasing the production of Death Magnetic, the band's ninth album and the first with Rob Trujillo on bass, was launched in May 2008.</br><br>
            In September 2008, the album was finally released and was praised for its sound. Many consider this album a return to the sound of the band's four classic albums released in the 1980s. The solos, which were absent in St. Anger, are back, and the speed of the songs and thrash metal, not seen since ...And Justice For All, are also present.</br><br>
            <br></p>
